/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - PRF is the number of pulses sent per second.
   - Runamb = c Tr / 2 = c / (2 PRF) Here Runamb is the maximum unambiguous range in m; Tr is in s; PRF is in Hz; c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s.
   - Turned round, a radar that must place targets correctly out to Rmax needs PRF ≤ c/(2Rmax): 150 km allows at most 1 kHz.
   - The fraction of time the transmitter is on is the duty cycle, d = τ/Tr = τ·PRF.
   - Ten times the PRF gives one tenth of the unambiguous range.
   - A target beyond Runamb still returns an echo, but it arrives after the next pulse has left.
   - Rapp is the apparent range; R is the true range; n is the whole number of intervals the echo is late, chosen so that 0 ≤ Rapp < Runamb.
   - The figure draws this for the worked example: a target at 200 km, at a PRF of 1 kHz, shows up at 50 km.
   - At the near end, the receiver is switched off while the pulse is sent and while the duplexer recovers.
   - Rmin is the minimum range in m; τ is the pulse width and trec the duplexer and receiver recovery time, both in s.
*/
