/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The echo from a target at range R has travelled 2R, so its phase lags the transmitter by φ = 2π(2R/λ) = 4πR/λ radians.
   - fd = 2 vr / λ = 2 vr f0 / c Here fd is the Doppler shift in Hz; vr is the radial velocity in m/s, the part of the target velocity along the line of sight (v cos θ for a target moving at angle θ to that line); λ = c/f0 is the wavelength in m
   - A closing target raises the echo frequency; an opening target lowers it.
   - A CW radar has one oscillator at f0 running continuously.
   - A CW radar cannot measure range: the wave has no timing mark.
   - An FMCW radar sweeps its frequency linearly by Δf over a time Tm.
   - fb is the beat frequency in Hz; R the range in m; Δf the sweep width in Hz; Tm the duration of one sweep in s.
   - For a triangular sweep with modulation frequency fm (one up-sweep and one down-sweep each period), each sweep lasts Tm = 1/(2fm) and the formula becomes fb = 4RΔf·fm/c, the form many textbooks print.
   - ΔR is the range resolution in m.
   - A moving target adds its Doppler shift to the beat.
*/
