/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Clutter can be 40 to 60 dB stronger than an aircraft echo, so no threshold can pass the aircraft and stop the ground.
   - A pulse radar sees Doppler as a change of phase from pulse to pulse.
   - The comparison is done separately for every range gate: the sample at delay 2R/c from one pulse is compared with the sample at the same delay from the previous pulse, exactly one pulse interval Tr earlier.
   - A single delay-line canceller subtracts the previous pulse's output from the present one.
   - |H(fd)| = 2 |sin(π fd Tr)| Here H is the voltage response of the canceller (input amplitude 1); fd is the Doppler shift in Hz; Tr = 1/PRF is the pulse interval in s.
   - The response is zero whenever fd = n·PRF: the target then moves a whole number of half-wavelengths between pulses, its phase returns to the same value, and it cancels like clutter.
   - vb is the n-th blind speed in m/s, n = 1, 2, 3, …; λ is the wavelength in m; PRF is in Hz.
   - The response peaks at 2 when fd is an odd multiple of PRF/2.
   - Staggered PRF changes Tr from pulse to pulse.
   - A pulse Doppler radar sends a burst of N coherent pulses on each beam position and passes each range gate through a bank of N Doppler filters, usually an FFT.
*/
